{"candidates":[{"claim":"An idempotent operation satisfies f(f(x))=f(x): repeated application converges to the same state as a single application. A safe operation merely guarantees that retries do not violate system invariants—it need not produce identical state, only correct state. An append-only event log requires safety of its writers, not idempotency, because append is inherently non-idempotent (appending event E twice yields [E,E], not [E]). The log survives at-least-once delivery by making retries safe through deterministic operation identifiers or deduplication keys, not by making the append operation itself idempotent.","counterconditions":["If the log has no deduplication layer and consumers cannot tolerate duplicate events, then safety alone is insufficient and the system cannot survive at-least-once delivery","If the deduplication key is not deterministic (e.g., a random UUID per attempt), retries produce distinct keys and the log cannot distinguish duplicates from genuinely new events","If the event payload itself is non-deterministic (e.g., includes wall-clock timestamps), then even with deduplication keys, replay may diverge because the first-delivered copy differs from the retried copy"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The append operation mutates the log by adding an entry. Calling append(E) twice produces two entries, violating f(f(x))=f(x). However, the writer can attach a deterministic, content-derived or operation-ID-based key to each event. On replay or retry, the log layer (or a downstream deduplication filter) recognizes duplicate keys and discards redundant entries. The system-level invariant—deterministic replay producing identical derived state—is preserved because the deduplication mechanism is itself idempotent, even though the raw append is not. Thus writers must be retry-safe (their retries produce detectable duplicates), and the log achieves safety, not idempotency, at the operation level.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.92},{"claim":"The distinction is best understood through counterexample. Consider three writers: (a) a writer that appends a random UUID each time—neither idempotent nor safe under retry; (b) a writer that appends a deterministic content hash each time—safe (duplicates are detectable and removable) but still not idempotent (append of the same hash twice still yields two log entries before dedup); (c) a writer that checks the log for an existing entry with the same key before appending—this makes the compound check-then-append operation idempotent in effect, but it requires a read-before-write that breaks the append-only model's single-direction discipline. The append-only log requires property (b): safety through deterministic identity, not the idempotency of (c), because (c) introduces a read path that contradicts the append-only discipline.","counterconditions":["If the log supports atomic compare-and-append (conditional append only if key absent), then writer (c) becomes feasible without a separate read path, and the distinction collapses","If the deduplication pass during replay is itself non-deterministic (e.g., depends on arrival order rather than key ordering), then writer (b) is not actually safe"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Writer (a) fails because duplicates are indistinguishable from new events. Writer (c) achieves idempotency by reading the log before appending, but this introduces a read-before-write coupling that violates the append-only principle and creates a race condition under concurrent writers. Writer (b) is safe because every retry carries a deterministic key; the log accepts all appends (preserving append-only discipline) and a separate deduplication pass during replay filters duplicates. The key insight is that safety is achievable within the append-only model, while true operation-level idempotency requires a read path that breaks it.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.78},{"claim":"Both properties are required, but at different architectural layers. The writer must be safe (retry attempts are harmless or detectably redundant), and the replay/deduplication layer must be idempotent in its effect on derived state. The distinction between idempotent and safe is thus a layer-boundary distinction: safety is the property demanded of the producer (it may append duplicates, but they must be identifiable), while idempotency is the property demanded of the consumer/replay engine (processing a duplicate must not change derived state beyond the first processing). The append-only log requires safety of its writers and idempotency of its readers, not idempotency of its writers.","counterconditions":["If the replay engine is not idempotent in its deduplication (e.g., it uses a Bloom filter that can produce false negatives), then duplicate events may slip through and corrupt derived state","If the writer's identity scheme is insufficient (e.g., keys are unique per process restart rather than per logical operation), safety is violated","If there is no clear boundary between writer and replay engine (e.g., in a single-process system), the layer distinction becomes ambiguous and the argument may not hold"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The system decomposes into a write path and a read/replay path. On the write path, at-least-once delivery means the writer may submit the same event multiple times. The writer must ensure each submission carries enough identity information (a deterministic operation ID) that duplicates can be recognized—this is safety, not idempotency, because the writer does not guarantee that two appends produce one entry. On the read/replay path, the deduplication filter ensures that processing the same key twice has the same effect as processing it once—this is idempotency, applied to the consumer's state transition function. The append-only log's contract is: writers provide safety (identifiable retries), the log provides the raw history, and the replay engine provides idempotency (deduplication).","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"claim":"The log actually requires idempotency of writers, not merely safety, because deterministic replay demands that the replayed state be exactly identical to the state that would have been produced without retries. Safety, which permits state divergence as long as invariants hold, is insufficient for deterministic replay: a safe-but-not-idempotent writer could produce log states [E] under no-retry and [E,E-dup] under retry, where E-dup is a deduplicated stub. Even if downstream consumers handle both correctly, the raw log itself differs, breaking byte-for-byte replay determinism. Only true idempotency—guaranteeing the writer's effect on the log is identical regardless of retry count—satisfies deterministic replay.","counterconditions":["If deterministic replay is defined at the semantic level (after dedup) rather than the byte level, then safety suffices and this claim is too strong","If the log is content-addressable and duplicate appends naturally map to the same storage location, then append becomes idempotent by construction and the distinction between safe and idempotent vanishes for this specific log type","If the replay engine is defined to normalize the log (strip duplicates) before replay, then the raw log need not be deterministic, only the normalized log"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Deterministic replay requires that the log's contents are a function of the sequence of logical operations, not of delivery retries. If a writer's retry can produce a different log (even if the different log is semantically equivalent after dedup), then replay is not byte-for-byte deterministic. The writer must therefore guarantee that appending operation O once and appending it twice produce the same log. This requires either conditional append (append only if key not present) or a content-addressable log where duplicate keys map to the same entry. In either case, the writer's effect on the log must be idempotent, not merely safe.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.35},{"claim":"The distinction between idempotent and safe is a false dichotomy for append-only logs. What the log actually requires of its writers is commutativity with respect to the deduplication function: appending E then E (retry) must yield the same deduplicated result as appending E then E in the opposite order, or appending E once and then the retry. The relevant property is neither idempotency (f(f(x))=f(x)) nor safety (invariants preserved), but rather that the deduplication function D applied to the log is a monotonically increasing fixed point: D(log + [E]) = D(log + [E, E]) = D(D(log + [E]) + [E]). The log requires writers whose operations commute under D.","counterconditions":["If D is not well-defined (e.g., deduplication depends on non-deterministic factors like network arrival order), then no writer property suffices","If D is stateful and depends on the entire log history rather than just the current event, then commutativity may not hold even for well-behaved writers","If the log has no deduplication function at all, then the requirement reduces to raw idempotency of append, which is impossible for a true append-only log"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Idempotency is a property of a single operation applied multiple times. Safety is a property of system invariants under retries. But the append-only log's real requirement is about the interaction between the operation and the deduplication function. If D is a well-defined deduplication function (e.g., keep first occurrence of each key, or keep last occurrence), then the writer's requirement is that D is a fixed point of repeated appends: D(L ++ [E] ++ [E]) = D(L ++ [E]). This is idempotency of D∘append, not of append alone. The writer must produce events for which D∘append is idempotent, which is a commutativity-like property between the operation and D, not a standalone property of the operation.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.18},{"claim":"In practice, append-only event logs such as Apache Kafka achieve at-least-once delivery safety through idempotent producers at the protocol level, but this is a misnomer: the producer's 'idempotency' is actually safety achieved via sequence numbers and transactional IDs that allow the broker to deduplicate. The append to the log is never idempotent—the broker still writes entries—it is the producer-broker protocol that is safe. Published definitions from the messaging literature (e.g., Burnstein & Shapiro on exactly-once semantics) distinguish idempotency as an application-level property from safety as a protocol-level property. The log requires safety of the writer-broker protocol, and idempotency is an emergent property of the consumer's state machine, not a requirement on writers.","counterconditions":["If the broker's sequence number tracking is lost (e.g., on broker failover before the state is replicated), the safety mechanism breaks and duplicates may appear in the log","If the consumer is not idempotent, protocol-level safety is insufficient for correct application semantics","If the definition of 'idempotent producer' in the Kafka documentation conflates protocol-level safety with operation-level idempotency, then the terminology distinction I draw may not match published usage"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Kafka's idempotent producer assigns a Producer ID (PID) and a monotonically increasing sequence number to each record. The broker tracks the largest sequence number per PID-partition and rejects duplicates. The append to the log is still non-idempotent (the broker writes every accepted record). What is 'idempotent' is the end-to-end effect: the consumer sees each logical message exactly once. This is safety at the protocol level (the broker's rejection of duplicates is the safety mechanism) combined with idempotency at the consumer level. The published distinction is that idempotency is about application semantics (processing a message twice has the same effect as once), while safety is about protocol guarantees (duplicates are detected and suppressed). The log requires the latter of its writers.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55}],"simulation_proposals":[]}